md's own build order has the conflict queue (`app/api/conflicts/route.ts`) filtering by `runId`, `severity`, `status`, and the audit view joining on `resolutionId`. Without indexes, \"filtered by severity and status\" over a run with thousands of conflicts is a sequential scan per request.\n\n```ts\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", { ... },\n  (table) => [index(\"matches_run_idx\").on(table.runId)],\n);\n\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", { ... },\n  (table) => [\n    index(\"conflicts_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n    index(\"conflicts_match_idx\").on(table.matchId),\n    index(\"conflicts_run_severity_idx\").on(table.runId, table.severity),\n  ],\n);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", { ... },\n  (table) => [\n    index(\"resolutions_conflict_idx\").on(table.conflictId),\n    index(\"resolutions_status_idx\").on(table.status),\n  ],\n);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", { ... },\n  (table) => [index(\"audit_log_run_idx\").on(table.runId)],\n);\n```\n\n### 3. The one N+1 to avoid when you build the conflict queue\n\nThe conflict queue diff row needs `conflict → match → both entities → resolution`. Do this as joins, not per-row fetches:\n\n```ts\n// avoid: one query per conflict inside a map()\nconst conflicts = await getDb().select().from(conflictsTable)...;\nfor (const c of conflicts) {\n  const match = await getDb().select().from(matches).where(eq(matches.id, c.matchId)); // N+1\n}\n\n// instead: single joined query\nconst rows = await getDb()\n  .select()\n  .from(conflicts)\n  .innerJoin(matches, eq(conflicts.matchId, matches.id))\n  .innerJoin(entities, or(eq(matches.entityAId, entities.id), eq(matches.entityBId, entities.id)))\n  .leftJoin(resolutions, eq(resolutions.conflictId, conflicts.id))\n  .where(eq(conflicts.runId, runId));\n```\n\n### 4. Caching — the one already specified but not yet built\n\nAGENTS.md already calls for prompt caching on the ruleset block (`engine/agent/graph.py`, \"single largest cost lever\") and it's still a stub. When you implement `propose`/`classify`, make sure the ruleset block is a stable, cached prefix in the Anthropic call (`cache_control: {type: \"ephemeral\"}` on that content block), not re-serialized per conflict — that's the real caching win in this codebase, not a web-layer memoization concern.\n\n### 5. React re-renders / memory leaks — none present\n\n`NavRail` and `SignOutControl` are the only client components with hooks (`usePathname`, `useUser`), no intervals, no subscriptions, no derived-state loops. Nothing to fix. Watch for this later: when you build run-status polling with TanStack Query (per AGENTS.md, the only place client fetching is allowed), set a `refetchInterval` that stops once `status` is terminal — otherwise it'll keep polling a finished run indefinitely:\n\n```ts\nuseQuery({\n  queryKey: [\"run\", runId, \"status\"],\n  queryFn: () => fetchRunStatus(runId),\n  refetchInterval: (query) =>\n    query.state.data?.status === \"complete\" || query.state.data?.status === \"failed\"\n      ? false\n      : 2000,\n});\n```\n\n### 6. Redundant computation — engine pipeline is unimplemented, nothing to profile\n\n`blocking.py`/`detect.py` are stubs. The one thing worth flagging in advance: `blocking.py` already documents \"never compare all pairs\" — good — but when you implement `score()`, don't recompute embeddings inside the scoring loop; embed once during `block()` and pass vectors through, not raw text, into `score()`.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line**: items 1 and 2 are real, actionable today. Items 3–6 are pre-emptive guidance tied to features not yet built — want me to apply the index migration (#2) and the `countSourcesBySide` fix (#1) now?\n",
